module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**unary minus absorbs a following additive operator** (invalid state: the seed parser reads `-1 - j` as `-(1 - j)` and `-a + b` as `-(a + b)`. v1.compiler.parse parse_prefix parses the operand of ShMinus at min_bp 12, while extdeps.languages.dag.syntax gives infix `+` and `-` left_bp 13 (and `*`, `/` 15), so the operand loop keeps consuming every additive and multiplicative operator after the prefix. Harm: silent wrongness. The program is accepted and computes a different integer, with no diagnostic on any route that reads the seed's parse tree, which is the interpreter and every emitter.)",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: a negative literal standing alone (`last_rank: -1`, `{ -1 }`) is unaffected, as is any unary minus whose operand is followed by a comparison, a logical operator or a closing bracket. Only an additive or multiplicative infix operator that follows the operand is absorbed. A multiplicative absorption (`-a * b` read as `-(a * b)`) agrees in value except at the overflow boundary, so the additive case is the one that changes answers.",

    "RECEIPT 2026-10-19 (std.integer Int delivery 1): the signed grounding fold first spelled the negative encoding `-1 - j`, and `Negative { pred: 2 }` emitted `1` instead of `-3`. The emitted-bytes witness test.claim.integer_signed_grounding_emit_witness caught it, and a probe module confirmed the interpreter agrees with the emitter: `-1 - 2 == 0 - 3` is false. The fold now spells `0 - 1 - k`. A corpus census at that head, over every .dag file outside the stage0 mirrors, found no other expression of the exposed shape (a unary minus in expression-start position whose operand is followed by an arithmetic infix operator), validated against the known positives above.",

    "Rung found at: silent wrongness, which is outside the ladder and forbidden. It is latent today only because the census found no exposed site, and nothing stops the next author writing one. Ceiling: structurally guaranteed. Prefix binding power is a modeled grammar fact, and a prefix operand bound above every infix operator it should not absorb cannot misparse. Next trigger, at capability grain: prefix operators' binding powers declared in the language's syntax spec beside OperatorSpec (not the literal 12 in parse_prefix), read by the seed parser and by v2's grammar-row parse alike. A discriminating fixture must show `-1 - 2` evaluating to -3 on the interpreter and in the emitted Rust, and the v2 native parse must be checked for the same reading before either is called fixed.",

    "FIX RUNG 2026-10-20 (lane adhoc-9102e2d6-92f, PR #14559's successor): the trigger fired and was paid. std.syntax declares PrefixOperatorSpec { symbol, binding_power } beside OperatorSpec and SyntaxSpec carries prefix_operators; v1.compiler.parse parse_prefix reads BOTH its arms (ShBang and ShMinus, the same rule in a sibling arm per DESIGN 6b) through find_prefix_binding_power from ctx.env.syntax_spec -- the same table the infix gate reads -- and a symbol with no declared row is not a prefix operator there; no literal binding power survives in the parser. extdeps.languages.dag.syntax and extdeps.languages.rust.syntax (the only two SyntaxSpec constructors) declare `-` and `!` at 18: above `|>` (17), below `.` (19), so the prefix binds exactly its postfix operand -- the closure the v2 grammar rows enforce structurally (dag_grammar_unary_expr_expr: unary_expr -> '-' unary_expr | postfix_expr, which cannot reach an infix row at any power).",

    "EXECUTED EVIDENCE FOR THE FIX RUNG (all at the PR head, remote runner, one dispatch; the shape probes ran the two parsers directly and the value claim ran the interpreter over its own parsed body): (1) v2 owed AGREEMENT, not a re-plumb, and it was executed first -- the v2 shape probe (14 arms) is green on main unchanged, including the boundary probes '-a.b' (dot consumed INSIDE the operand by the postfix layer) and '-a |> f' (pipe kept OUTSIDE), which is what pinned the declared power at 18 rather than 20; the v1 shape probe reads 4 arms BAD on main (the RED: '-1 - 2' shaped as the prefix over the whole additive row) and 14 OK post-fix. (2) The v1-vs-v2 agreement claim runs the same texts through both parsers and requires identical nesting AND the independently-stated expected shape, defect cases and controls: -1 - 2, -(1 - 2), -a + b, -a * b, -1 < 0, standalone -3, !a && b, !a || b, !a * b, plain a - b, a * b, -a < b. (3) The value claim evaluates on the v1 interpreter: -1 - 2 == -3, -1 + 2 == 1, !t && f == false, !t || t == true, the triple -1 - 2 - 3 == -6 (the old binding computes 4), controls -(1 - 2) == 1, -1 * 2 == -2, -1 < 0, -3 == 0 - 3; pre-fix the same claim refuses with -1 - 2 = 1 -- it is the RED. (4) The emitted-Rust channel: the emitter renders the triple's tree in text -- post-fix the outer additive row is over the negated literal ('(-1 - 2) - (3)'), pre-fix the minus covers the row ('-(1 - 2) - (3)'); the pair renders identically under both trees (Neg does not parenthesize), so the triple is the discriminator. (5) CENSUS AT MAIN, the silent-meaning-change ruling-out: a scratch bin over the mirror parser walked every authored tree in dag/ and src/ (2050 .dag files, 0 parse failures) and found ZERO prefix nodes whose operand root is +,-,*,/,% -- zero authored expressions change meaning under the fix -- and 133 prefix+method-call-root sites, every one a parenthesized or postfix operand that the declared power leaves exactly as it was. (6) The seed mirror is regenerated with claim_executor --regen-round-cost over the present files, the round diff carries parse_prefix's declared-power read, and round 2 writes 0 files.",

    "NAMED SINGLE-AUTHORITY DEBT left by this rung: TWO operator authorities still exist -- v1 reads SyntaxSpec.prefix_operators on the 5..20 scale (extdeps.languages.dag.syntax), v2 reads its grammar-row binding powers on the 1..7 scale (extdeps.languages.dag dag_infix_row). They agree where it matters (this row's executed agreement claim is the guard), but one table on one scale is the ceiling; the trigger for paying it is the day the two tables must move together (an operator added to one and not the other starts failing the agreement claim, which names this row).",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
